<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">DP World</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">DP World is a multinational port and logistics company based in Dubai, United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, formed in 2005 by merging the Dubai Ports Authority and Dubai Ports International. It operates container terminals on six continents, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-ali">Jebel Ali</a> in Dubai. In 2016 it signed a 30-year concession to develop and run the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dp-world.jpg" alt="DP World" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DP World · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DP_World_2021_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>One of its two parents, Dubai Ports International, was founded in 1999. Its first project was the South Container Terminal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> in Saudi Arabia, and it went on to run the ports of Djibouti from 2000, Vizag in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 2002 and Constanța in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> from 2003. In January 2005 it bought CSX World Terminals, and in September 2005 it merged with the Dubai Ports Authority. Sultan Ahmed bin Sulayem became chairman in 2007 and is credited with turning a regional port operator into a global logistics firm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In March 2006 DP World bought P&amp;O, then the fourth largest port operator in the world, for £3.9 billion. P&amp;O's terminals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, Baltimore, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> and Miami drew objections over port security from members of both parties in Congress, although President George W. Bush threatened to veto any bill blocking the deal. On 16 March 2006 the House of Representatives voted 348 to 71 for blocking it, and DP World sold the American operations to a division of American International Group.</p><p>In November 2007 it sold 20 per cent of its shares on NASDAQ Dubai in what was then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>'s largest initial public offering, raising 4.96 billion dollars. Moody's cut its rating to junk after the Dubai debt standstill of 2009 and restored it to investment grade by April 2011. Work on the London Gateway port began in 2010. The company bought back P&amp;O Ferries in 2019, and on 17 March 2022 P&amp;O Ferries dismissed 800 British seafarers by video call without consultation, which government and business leaders criticised. In November 2023 a cyberattack halted its Australian terminals for several days and left a backlog of 30,000 containers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Alongside port terminals the company runs cargo logistics, maritime services and free trade zones, and since 2016 it has bought businesses along the supply chain. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> it holds 51 per cent of the project, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> holding 30 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> 19 under an agreement of 1 March 2018, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> tried without success to block the deal. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> it began building a deep-water port at Ndayane, south of Dakar, in January 2022.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “DP World” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2016 the Dubai company <b>DP World</b> took a concession to operate and expand Berbera port, opening a new container terminal in 2021, and the Berbera corridor to Ethiopia has become central to hopes of economic growth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Emirates and Etihad made Dubai and Abu Dhabi hubs of long-haul aviation, and <b>DP World</b>, the Dubai port operator, runs terminals on several continents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
